            <ArticleTitle>Coronary dissection healing patterns: from complete resolution to restenosis, insights from optical coherence tomography.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>270-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4244/EIJV7I2A43</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">EIJV7I2A43</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 68-year-old patient presented a long, type C coronary dissection after balloon angioplasty of a focal lesion in a distal marginal branch. Due to the small vessel size the dissection was left untreated. At angiographic follow-up the dissection had completely disappeared, but a localised restenosis was found in the proximal vessel. Optical coherence tomography (OCT) confirmed the complete healing of the dissection and revealed a normal vessel wall (restitutio ad integrum). This technique also provided unique insights into the underlying substrate of the proximal restenosis unravelling a complicated plaque with associated thrombus.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
